<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
  <false/>
  <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
  <array/>
  <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
  <array>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhoneNumber</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeContacts</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailsOrTextMessages</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <!-- The area's name the person saves on Nudj's day-starts card, typed or filled in by
         "Use my location". It is coarse location either way, which was already true of a typed
         area.
         STILL NOT PreciseLocation, and build 8.9.1 does not change that although the prompt now
         opens on Precise (NSLocationDefaultAccuracyReduced is gone, so arrival reminders can be
         watched against a real fix). What is COLLECTED is still only the area's NAME: no
         coordinate is stored or sent, by this app or by anyone. Arrival reminders add nothing
         here either — the system watches the place and shows the reminder, the app is not woken,
         and X0 Bot is never told where the person is or that they arrived. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeCoarseLocation</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <!-- Health: Apple Health read only, for a fitness bot the person made and switched on: daily
         step totals, workouts (kind and minutes) and, from 8.9.1 and only if they turn it on,
         MINUTES ASLEEP PER NIGHT. No sleep stages, no bed or wake times. Kept 31 days and
         deleted when the switch goes off. Nothing else is read, nothing is written, and none of
         it is ever used for ads or sent to an AI service. Both rows come out again if iPhone
         health is cut. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeHealth</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <!-- Fitness: the same daily step totals and workout minutes, and from 8.9.1 also the ones
         Motion & Fitness counts when a person picks it instead of Apple Health. Apple's Fitness
         type names the Motion and Fitness API itself, so this row covers both sources; the
         payload is identical either way. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeFitness</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhotosorVideos</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurchaseHistory</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherFinancialInfo</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeAudioData</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <!-- Other User Content: what the person writes to their bots and their own plan. From 8.9.1
         this row also covers the BUSY TIMES read from the iPhone's calendars (start, end and
         all-day only — never an event's name, place, notes, people or calendar name) and the
         items of the one Reminders list X0 Bot makes, which become Nudj to-dos and are kept
         like typed ones. Busy times are replaced at each sync, deleted when the switch goes off,
         and gone a day after each block ends. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
        <!-- Product Personalization too, which is what the App Privacy answers have always said
             (docs/store-privacy.md §3). The bots' shared memory is written from what the person
             tells them and is read back to answer in their terms, so this content does shape what
             they are shown — "App Functionality" alone would have been the narrower answer, and
             the two have to agree because the manifest ships in the binary. -->
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeProductPersonalization</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <!-- Crash Data: Sentry. LINKED, as the App Privacy answers say. sentry.ts sets no user, but a
         crash report carries the account's id in its breadcrumbs and tags often enough that "not
         linked" is not an honest answer (the deletion screen says so too: deleteAccount.ts). -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeCrashData</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <!-- Performance Data: Sentry traces and the keyboard's counters. LINKED, for the same reason
         as Crash Data above. Both purposes stay: App Functionality (a slow screen is fixed from
         these) and Analytics. App Store Connect needs App Functionality adding beside Analytics,
         which is the owner half of this change. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePerformanceData</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <!-- BUILD 8.9.1: ABUSE AND FRAUD PREVENTION, and only that (perms2/SPEC.md 8, 8.7).
         NSPrivacyTracking stays false above and every one of these three is Tracking false, which
         is the whole point: none of it goes to Meta, an advertiser, analytics or
         personalisation, and none of it is ever used to follow a person across apps or sites.

         Device ID: the random number the app makes on first launch and keeps in AsyncStorage
         (it resets if the app is reinstalled, and nothing about the device goes into making it),
         and App Attest's key id. Apple's App Functionality purpose covers preventing fraud and
         implementing security measures. NO IDFA, NO IDFV, and no id built from a device or
         network trait: Apple names the network connection among its fingerprinting examples, so
         the trust code reads nothing about the network at all. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <!-- Other Diagnostic Data: the yes/no answers Apple's App Attest and DeviceCheck and
         Google's Play Integrity give about whether this is the real app on a real device, and the
         short reason code when one of them cannot answer. The ANSWER is kept, never the token.
         Analytics is here too, matching docs/store-privacy.md, which has declared this type since
         before the manifest did. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDiagnosticData</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <!-- Other Data Types: the two one-bit answers Nudj's calls need and nothing else —
         whether a Focus (or Do Not Disturb) is on, so a call becomes a notice, and whether the
         phone is in a car just before a call rings, so the call can wait. Never WHICH Focus,
         never a place, never a speed. The Focus state lapses after 12 hours; the driving answer
         is not kept past that call's own record. -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDataTypes</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
      <true/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
      <false/>
      <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
      <array>
        <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
  </array>
  <!-- REQUIRED-REASON APIs. NOTHING IS ADDED HERE IN 8.9.1, and that is a constraint on the code,
       not an oversight. The App Group UserDefaults that X0 Bot's Focus Filter writes is already
       covered by 1C8F.1 below, which is listed. The four reasons below — SystemBootTime 35F9.1,
       DiskSpace E174.1, FileTimestamp C617.1 and the two ActiveKeyboards reasons — are each
       declared for the app's own purposes, and NONE of them covers abuse or fraud prevention. So
       the trust code may not touch any of those APIs: no boot time, no disk space, no file
       timestamps, no keyboard list, and no IDFV. A phone test scans the trust files for every one
       of those names (perms2/SPEC.md 8.2 rule 7, 10.5, 12). -->
  <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
  <array>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
      <array>
        <string>CA92.1</string>
        <!-- 1C8F.1: the App Group shared by the app, the keyboard and the widgets. -->
        <string>1C8F.1</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
      <array>
        <string>C617.1</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
      <array>
        <string>35F9.1</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
      <array>
        <string>E174.1</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryActiveKeyboards</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
      <array>
        <string>54BD.1</string>
        <!-- 3EC4.1: a custom keyboard app checking whether its keyboard is on. -->
        <string>3EC4.1</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
